# Non-owning native-screen mount implementation

Status: not started

Related design: `docs/non-owning-native-screen-mount-proposal.md`

## Purpose

Extend the existing `ds.mount()` lifecycle so a call with no component attaches
non-owningly to the current native DF viewscreen. The attachment must expose
base-game native widgets and registered overlays through the existing fluent
subject API while preserving the real viewscreen, focus, screen stack, pointer
state, and normal DFHack input and rendering paths.

This checklist is the implementation completion contract. A phase is complete
only when every subtask and exit-gate item in that phase is checked and its
evidence is recorded in the evidence ledger.

## Scope boundary

Included:

- a no-component overload of `ds.mount()`;
- a borrowed, pinned native viewscreen interaction target;
- `viewscreen.widgets` as the default native subject root;
- adapter-backed subjects for native DF userdata and Lua `gui.View` tables;
- native lookup by widget name and zero-based child index;
- explicit lookup of enabled overlay-registry widgets;
- native widget inspection, bounds, text, state, and bounded tree capture;
- subject-relative and absolute-coordinate pointer placement;
- native input and mouse input through normal DFHack dispatch;
- reversible native render observation for waitable redraws;
- deterministic stale-screen, stale-subject, and cleanup behavior;
- Lua declarations, public documentation, unit coverage, and live DFHack
  validation.

Excluded:

- creating or showing a `ZScreen` for a native attachment;
- dismissing, replacing, resizing, or navigating the borrowed native screen;
- creating, owning, enabling, disabling, or repositioning native widgets or
  registered overlays;
- semantic aliases for unstable or unnamed base-game controls;
- CSS-like selectors, query languages, or fuzzy widget lookup;
- promising text extraction for every native widget type;
- calling native widget callbacks or overlay `onInput()` methods directly;
- supporting a DFHack Lua screen that currently owns focus above the native
  screen;
- changing the `dfhack-run` transport, scheduler, run history, or persistence;
- designing or implementing the separate in-game test-runner UI;
- adding a second public native-mount command;
- tests whose only purpose is to restate rockspec or VS Code task contents.

## Locked implementation contract

☐ `ds.mount()` with no component attaches to the current native DF viewscreen.
☐ `ds.mount(component, options)` preserves its current component behavior and
  overload resolution.
☐ A native attachment owns zero screens and never calls screen show, dismiss,
  resize, replace, or navigation operations.
☐ Attachment succeeds only when `dfhack.gui.getCurViewscreen(true)` and
  `dfhack.gui.getDFViewscreen(true)` identify the same screen.
☐ The exact attached screen is pinned for the lifetime of the mount.
☐ The exact `attached_screen.widgets` container is the default subject root.
☐ `ds.root():raw()` returns that exact typed DF widget container.
☐ Existing component subjects continue returning their exact Lua view tables.
☐ `ds.get()` accepts existing string paths and a path-segment array.
☐ Native path segments are nonempty strings or nonnegative, integral,
  zero-based child indices.
☐ The segment-array form is authoritative for native names containing `/`.
☐ Native widget traversal delegates to `dfhack.gui.getWidget()` and
  `dfhack.gui.getWidgetChildren()`.
☐ Native lookup does not filter invisible, inactive, or otherwise
  non-interactable widgets.
☐ `ESubjectSource` is an immutable enum created with
  `dwarfspec.immutable_enum.define`.
☐ `ESubjectSource.NATIVE` is the default source for a native attachment.
☐ `ESubjectSource.OVERLAY` requires an exact enabled overlay registry name.
☐ Overlay registry names remain validated runtime strings, not enum members.
☐ `ds.root()`, `ds.get()`, and `ds.capture_view_tree()` use the same optional
  subject-source contract.
☐ A `Subject` stores an adapter-backed descriptor and does not require its raw
  target to be a Lua table.
☐ Native `Subject:raw()` returns typed DF userdata; Lua-view
  `Subject:raw()` returns the actual Lua table.
☐ Retained native subjects reacquire their path and validate captured identity
  before every inspection or interaction.
☐ A replacement at the same native or overlay path makes the retained subject
  stale; it is never silently rebound.
☐ Every native operation validates that the pinned viewscreen is still current
  before dereferencing a native widget.
☐ `ds.input()`, `ds.mouseInput()`, subject input, and subject clicks dispatch
  through the pinned native viewscreen.
☐ Subjects provide geometry and diagnostic context but never bypass normal
  native or overlay input routing.
☐ `ds.move_pointer(x, y)` accepts integral, zero-based, in-window native screen
  coordinates.
☐ Subject-relative pointer operations use current normalized widget bounds.
☐ `ds.redraw()` and `Subject:redraw()` wait for a completed render by default.
☐ `{wait=false}` invalidates without waiting for render completion.
☐ Native render completion is observed after the normal overlay render
  dispatcher has completed successfully for the pinned screen.
☐ Pointer, button, render-observer, subject, and mount state restore on normal
  unmount and every failure path.
☐ Cleanup never dismisses or otherwise mutates the borrowed native screen.

## Global engineering rules

☐ Add LuaDoc comments to every new or changed method, function, class, adapter,
  record, option table, and public type.
☐ Keep public behavior in `ds.lua`; keep native attachment, widget adaptation,
  text extraction, and render observation in focused modules.
☐ Do not add scattered `type(value) == 'userdata'` branches as a substitute for
  the subject-adapter boundary.
☐ Keep adapters read-only except for operations explicitly delegated to the
  interaction target or pointer adapter.
☐ Prefer dependency-injected DFHack services in unit-testable modules.
☐ Keep failure messages explicit, source-aware, path-aware, and bounded.
☐ Do not recursively dump arbitrary DF userdata in diagnostics.
☐ Preserve unrelated user changes and existing worktree state.
☐ Record syntax, focused unit, full unit, packaging, and live DFHack evidence
  separately; one evidence layer does not imply another passed.
☐ Do not add packaging-contract tests or tests that merely duplicate static
  package configuration.
☐ Use Mermaid for any graph added to supporting documentation.

## Phase 0: Baseline and native-widget characterization

### Goal

Establish a reproducible baseline and record the current component-mount,
subject, pointer, redraw, and DFHack widget contracts before changing shared
infrastructure.

### Tasks

☐ Confirm the repository root and Git identity before editing.
☐ Record the initial `git status --short` output and preserve unrelated
  changes.
☐ Run the repository Lua syntax check and record its exact command and result.
☐ Run the full standalone unit suite and record its exact command and result.
☐ Identify every current use of `mount.host_screen` and classify it as screen
  ownership, interaction routing, subject rooting, rendering, or cleanup.
☐ Identify every current table-only assumption in `subject.lua`,
  `mount_context.lua`, diagnostics, and tree capture.
☐ Record the existing public and internal behavior of:
  `ds.mount(component)`, `ds.root()`, `ds.get()`, `Subject:raw()`,
  `ds.input()`, `ds.mouseInput()`, `ds.move_pointer()`, and `ds.redraw()`.
☐ Verify the target DFHack API names and shapes used by the proposal:
  `getCurViewscreen`, `getDFViewscreen`, `getWidget`,
  `getWidgetChildren`, `viewscreen.widgets`, widget flags, widget rectangles,
  and overlay render dispatch.
☐ Define injectable fixture boundaries for native userdata identity, widget
  hierarchy, overlay registry state, native screen identity, pointer state, and
  render generations.
☐ List the existing focused test files that must remain green while the shared
  subject and mount internals are refactored.

### Phase 0 exit gate

☐ Baseline syntax and unit results are recorded.
☐ Current ownership and table-only assumptions are documented in the evidence
  ledger.
☐ Native DFHack API assumptions have either been verified or converted into
  explicit capability checks.
☐ No production behavior has changed.

## Phase 1: Separate ownership, interaction, and subject adaptation

### Goal

Refactor the shared mount and subject internals so screen ownership,
interaction routing, and subject representation are independent concepts while
preserving all existing component behavior.

### Tasks

☐ Define a documented interaction-target contract with native-screen access,
  current-target validation, invalidation, and cleanup behavior.
☐ Define a documented subject-adapter contract for root resolution, traversal,
  identity, containment, children, names, native type, bounds, state, text,
  tooltip, and optional inspection fields.
☐ Replace the raw-table-only `Subject` payload with a documented descriptor
  containing mount identity, source, path segments, adapter, captured identity,
  and diagnostic path.
☐ Refactor `Subject` methods to resolve through the selected adapter before
  reading or interacting with the target.
☐ Preserve the fluent public subject API and current failure behavior where
  the proposal does not deliberately change it.
☐ Implement the Lua-view adapter over current component behavior:
  `subviews`, `view_id`, `frame_body`, evaluated `visible`/`active`, text, and
  existing diagnostics.
☐ Refactor existing component mounts to provide an interaction target and Lua
  subject source while retaining their owned `ZScreen`.
☐ Ensure only screens actually created by DwarfSpec enter
  `mount_context.owned_screens`.
☐ Preserve current component show, resize, render instrumentation, dismissal,
  and cleanup behavior.
☐ Add focused regression tests for component root lookup, nested paths,
  retained subjects, raw access, input, redraw, and cleanup.
☐ Add LuaDoc annotations for every changed contract and implementation.

### Phase 1 exit gate

☐ All existing component-mount and subject tests pass without weakened
  assertions.
☐ Component raw subjects still return exact Lua tables.
☐ Ownership evidence still reports and cleans up DwarfSpec-owned component
  screens correctly.
☐ No native attachment behavior is exposed publicly yet.

## Phase 2: Subject-source enum and path contracts

### Goal

Introduce the public source and path types needed by native mounts without
using loose strings for behavior identifiers.

### Tasks

☐ Define immutable `ESubjectSource` values `NATIVE` and `OVERLAY` with
  `dwarfspec.immutable_enum.define`.
☐ Export `ESubjectSource` from the same public surface as the existing DwarfSpec
  enums.
☐ Validate source values by enum membership and reject arbitrary strings.
☐ Define a native path segment as either a nonempty string or a nonnegative
  integral number.
☐ Normalize string and segment-array paths without splitting names supplied as
  array segments.
☐ Preserve existing slash-delimited component path behavior.
☐ Define an explicit error for an ambiguous native string path whose intended
  widget name contains `/`, directing callers to segment-array syntax.
☐ Add optional source selection to the internal root, get, and tree-capture
  request shapes.
☐ Require an exact nonempty overlay name when `OVERLAY` is selected.
☐ Reject overlay options when they conflict with `NATIVE` selection.
☐ Add focused tests for valid enum members, invalid values, omitted defaults,
  path normalization, zero-based numeric segments, invalid segment types,
  negative/fractional indices, empty names, and names containing `/`.

### Phase 2 exit gate

☐ Source selection cannot be controlled by unvalidated identifier strings.
☐ Path normalization preserves existing component semantics.
☐ Native segment arrays preserve names containing `/` exactly.
☐ Focused enum and path-contract tests pass.

## Phase 3: Non-owning native attachment

### Goal

Implement the no-component mount path and its borrowed native interaction
target without creating, showing, or owning a screen.

### Tasks

☐ Route only a truly missing component argument to native attachment; preserve
  existing validation for invalid explicit component values.
☐ Acquire both the current viewscreen and native DF viewscreen through
  injectable DFHack calls.
☐ Require both calls to identify the exact same viewscreen.
☐ Reject a DFHack Lua screen that currently owns focus above the native
  viewscreen with an explicit error.
☐ Require a valid native `widgets` container before creating the mount record.
☐ Pin the exact native viewscreen identity and widget-root identity.
☐ Create a native interaction target whose `assert_current()` rejects any
  subsequent screen transition.
☐ Leave `mount.host_screen` unset for the native attachment.
☐ Add zero screens to `mount_context.owned_screens`.
☐ Return a subject whose raw value is the exact pinned `widgets` container.
☐ Reject `ds.viewport()` for a borrowed native screen with an explicit
  non-owning-mount error.
☐ Ensure failed attachment releases any partially installed run-scoped state.
☐ Add injected unit tests proving no screen construction, show, resize,
  dismiss, replace, or navigation function is called.
☐ Add tests for missing current screen, mismatched current/native screens,
  missing widget root, successful attachment, and immediate screen staleness.

### Phase 3 exit gate

☐ `ds.mount()` returns the exact native widget root in injected tests.
☐ Native mount evidence reports zero DwarfSpec-owned screens.
☐ All rejection paths leave screen, focus, stack, and ownership state
  unchanged.
☐ Component-mount regression tests remain green.

## Phase 4: Native widget traversal, identity, and liveness

### Goal

Make native widgets first-class retained subjects with deterministic lookup and
staleness rules.

### Tasks

☐ Implement a native widget adapter rooted at the exact pinned
  `viewscreen.widgets` container.
☐ Resolve each path segment through `dfhack.gui.getWidget()` one segment at a
  time.
☐ Enumerate ordered children with `dfhack.gui.getWidgetChildren()`.
☐ Support exact native names and zero-based child indices.
☐ Preserve and display the complete requested path in diagnostics.
☐ On lookup failure, report the failing segment, resolved parent name, native
  parent type, and bounded named/indexed child summaries.
☐ Capture the native typed-reference identity when a subject is created.
☐ Retain the subject locator strongly enough to reacquire a Lua userdata
  wrapper for each operation.
☐ Validate current-screen identity before reacquiring or dereferencing a native
  widget.
☐ Require the reacquired widget to equal the captured typed-reference identity.
☐ Mark a subject stale when its widget is removed or replaced at the same path.
☐ Require callers to use a new `ds.get()` after a replacement.
☐ Make `Subject:raw()` return the exact reacquired typed DF userdata.
☐ Keep invisible and inactive descendants discoverable.
☐ Add tests for named lookup, indexed lookup, mixed paths, `/` in a name,
  missing segments, bounded diagnostics, invisible/inactive targets, removal,
  replacement, screen transition, and exact raw identity.

### Phase 4 exit gate

☐ Native lookup and child diagnostics are deterministic and bounded.
☐ No native widget is dereferenced before current-screen validation.
☐ Retained subjects cannot silently change identity.
☐ Focused traversal and liveness tests pass.

## Phase 5: Native inspection, bounds, text, and tree capture

### Goal

Expose useful native widget state through the existing inspection and
diagnostics APIs without guessing or dumping arbitrary userdata.

### Tasks

☐ Normalize native bounds to integral zero-based inclusive
  `x1, y1, x2, y2` coordinates.
☐ Prefer the bound `widget:get_rect()` method when available and otherwise use
  `widget.rect`.
☐ Reject subject-relative pointer interaction for empty, invalid, or wholly
  off-window rectangles while keeping the subject inspectable.
☐ Map direct visibility and activity from
  `widget.flag.VISIBILITY_VISIBLE` and
  `widget.flag.VISIBILITY_ACTIVE`.
☐ If effective state is exposed, calculate it through the native parent chain
  and keep it distinct from direct flags.
☐ Implement a documented, type-aware native text-extractor registry.
☐ Add extractors for widget text, truncated text, multiline text, textbox text,
  and safe better-button display text.
☐ Aggregate visible textual descendants only when a direct text value is
  unavailable and the result remains bounded.
☐ Return `nil` for unsupported text types instead of reading rendered screen
  pixels or invoking callbacks.
☐ Expose documented optional native fields for type, name, tooltip, scroll
  position, visible-row count, and applicable selection state.
☐ Ensure read-only inspection never invokes activation, mutation, selection,
  or input callbacks.
☐ Generalize diagnostics and `capture_view_tree()` through the selected subject
  adapter.
☐ Bound tree depth, child count, text length, and diagnostic value size.
☐ Add tests for rectangle fallback and validation, direct/effective state,
  every supported text extractor, unsupported text, tooltip, scroll and
  selection fields, aggregate text, callback non-invocation, and bounded tree
  capture.

### Phase 5 exit gate

☐ Native inspection returns documented normalized values without arbitrary
  userdata traversal.
☐ Unsupported widget types remain safely inspectable.
☐ Native tree capture is deterministic and bounded.
☐ Focused inspection and diagnostics tests pass.

## Phase 6: Overlay-registry subject source

### Goal

Expose live registered overlays as an explicit secondary subject source while
leaving their lifecycle and normal dispatch under DFHack control.

### Tasks

☐ Read overlay state through `require('plugins.overlay').get_state()`.
☐ Resolve only the exact requested overlay registry entry.
☐ Require the requested overlay to be enabled and backed by a valid Lua view.
☐ Pin the exact registry name and Lua widget identity.
☐ Delegate overlay traversal, inspection, and raw access to the Lua-view
  adapter.
☐ Revalidate the registry name-to-widget mapping before every subject
  operation.
☐ Make existing overlay subjects stale after removal, disablement, or rescan
  replacement.
☐ Do not silently bind a stale subject to a new overlay widget with the same
  registry name.
☐ Keep overlay subjects as separate roots rather than children of the native
  widget tree.
☐ Ensure selecting an overlay source does not enable, disable, configure,
  position, or take ownership of the overlay.
☐ Add tests for default native selection, explicit overlay selection, missing
  names, disabled overlays, native/overlay path-name collisions, exact raw
  identity, rescan replacement, and source-specific tree capture.

### Phase 6 exit gate

☐ Native and overlay sources are explicit and cannot be confused by path
  collisions.
☐ Overlay subjects preserve Lua-view behavior and exact identity.
☐ Overlay lifecycle remains externally owned.
☐ Focused source-selection tests pass.

## Phase 7: Native input and pointer routing

### Goal

Route keyboard and mouse interactions through the pinned native viewscreen
while retaining subject geometry and exact pointer cleanup.

### Tasks

☐ Resolve all top-level and subject input commands through
  `mount.interaction_target`.
☐ Dispatch keyboard and mouse keys with
  `gui.simulateInput(attached_native_screen, keys)`.
☐ Preserve normal overlay interposition and unhandled native-screen input
  fall-through.
☐ Never call a native widget callback or overlay `onInput()` directly.
☐ Extend `ds.move_pointer()` with the `(x, y)` overload.
☐ Validate coordinate arguments as integral zero-based coordinates within the
  current window.
☐ Use normalized subject bounds for named pointer positions and clicks.
☐ Include subject source, path, native type, and bounds in pointer failure
  diagnostics where available.
☐ Preserve the existing `EMouseButton` and `EInputState` contracts, including
  default `CLICK` behavior.
☐ Route click, explicit down, explicit up, and wheel input at the currently set
  pointer position.
☐ Preserve virtual pointer, GPS pointer, mouse focus/tracking, and button state
  through the existing pointer adapter.
☐ Wait for completed rendering after interaction when the existing command
  contract requires it.
☐ Add tests for top-level input, subject input, native clicks, overlay clicks,
  arbitrary coordinates, each pointer position, invalid bounds, invalid
  coordinates, click/down/up/wheel states, overlay fall-through, screen
  staleness, and pointer restoration after injected failures.

### Phase 7 exit gate

☐ Native and overlay interactions both traverse the pinned native screen's
  normal input path.
☐ Arbitrary and subject-derived coordinates reach the existing pointer state
  consistently.
☐ All pointer and button state restores on success and failure.
☐ Focused input and pointer tests pass.

## Phase 8: Native render observation and redraw

### Goal

Provide a reversible completion signal for native-plus-overlay rendering so
redraw remains waitable without wrapping or owning the native screen.

### Tasks

☐ Implement a run-scoped observer around
  `plugins.overlay.render_viewscreen_widgets`.
☐ Capture the exact original dispatcher before replacement.
☐ Forward original arguments unchanged.
☐ Preserve all return values and error behavior from the original dispatcher.
☐ Advance the render tracker only after successful dispatch for the pinned
  native viewscreen.
☐ Report intercepted render failures through the existing render-tracker
  failure path.
☐ Ignore render dispatch for unrelated viewscreens.
☐ Restore the exact original dispatcher during cleanup.
☐ Detect a conflicting dispatcher replacement before restoration and report an
  explicit cleanup failure without overwriting unknown third-party state.
☐ Ensure the observer is compatible with the service's one-active-run
  execution rule and does not change cross-project queue semantics.
☐ On attachment, invalidate once and wait for a completed observed render as a
  capability check.
☐ Fail attachment explicitly if overlay dispatch is unavailable or completion
  cannot be observed.
☐ Implement native invalidation with `dfhack.screen.invalidate()`.
☐ Preserve the render-generation sequence: capture, invalidate, yield, observe
  completion, then resume.
☐ Preserve `{wait=false}` as an explicit invalidation-only path.
☐ Add tests for successful completion, unrelated-screen dispatch, multiple
  returns, original errors, observation errors, timeout, wait opt-out,
  conflicting replacement, exact restoration, and failed attachment cleanup.

### Phase 8 exit gate

☐ `ds.redraw()` waits for the pinned native screen's completed
  native-plus-overlay render boundary.
☐ `{wait=false}` does not enter the render wait path.
☐ Observer installation and restoration are exact and failure-safe.
☐ Focused render-observer and redraw tests pass.

## Phase 9: Cleanup, failure recovery, and ownership evidence

### Goal

Make every terminal path release DwarfSpec state while proving that the native
screen and externally owned overlays were never owned or dismissed.

### Tasks

☐ Register cleanup actions in LIFO order for pointer/button state, native
  render observation, subject descriptors, and mount attachment.
☐ Restore pointer and explicit button state before removing render
  observation.
☐ Clear all retained native and overlay subject descriptors during unmount.
☐ Release the borrowed attachment without registering a screen dismissal.
☐ Preserve exact cleanup behavior for normal unmount, assertion failure,
  input error, render error, redraw timeout, scheduler abort, and runner
  recovery.
☐ If input changes the current viewscreen, reject further operations without
  navigating back during cleanup.
☐ Avoid dereferencing retained native userdata after a screen transition.
☐ Extend cleanup and leak evidence so a native mount explicitly reports zero
  DwarfSpec-owned screens.
☐ Keep test-owned overlay registration cleanup separate from native mount
  cleanup.
☐ Include mount kind, source, path, and relevant captured/current identities in
  stale-state diagnostics without unbounded userdata serialization.
☐ Add tests for every cleanup entry point and for injected failures at each
  partially completed attachment step.
☐ Add repeated mount/unmount tests that prove no observer, pointer, subject, or
  ownership state accumulates.

### Phase 9 exit gate

☐ Cleanup tests cover every installed piece of reversible state.
☐ Native mounts always report zero owned screens and zero screen dismissals.
☐ Screen transitions produce explicit stale errors and no navigation.
☐ Repeated native attachment leaves no DwarfSpec state behind.

## Phase 10: Public declarations and documentation

### Goal

Document the finished behavior precisely for Lua authors without mixing this
runtime feature with the separate runner UI.

### Tasks

☐ Add the no-component `DS.mount()` overload to `src/ds.d.lua`.
☐ Declare native path segment and segment-array path types.
☐ Declare immutable `ESubjectSource` and its `NATIVE` and `OVERLAY` members.
☐ Declare source-option types for `DS.root()`, `DS.get()`, and
  `DS.capture_view_tree()`.
☐ Document that native `Subject:raw()` returns typed DF userdata and Lua-view
  subjects return Lua tables.
☐ Add documented optional native inspection fields without exposing an
  unbounded raw-widget map.
☐ Declare and document the `DS.move_pointer(x, y)` overload.
☐ Document wait-by-default redraw and `{wait=false}` for native mounts.
☐ Document the non-owning screen, overlay, staleness, and cleanup contracts.
☐ Add examples for native named paths, numeric path segments, names containing
  `/`, overlay source selection, arbitrary coordinates, input, mouse input, and
  redraw.
☐ Document explicit failure cases and the requirement to remount after a
  viewscreen transition.
☐ Keep runner-service and runner-UI documentation unchanged except for a
  narrowly scoped cross-reference if one is genuinely required.
☐ Review every new or changed API declaration against the implementation and
  LuaDoc comments.

### Phase 10 exit gate

☐ `src/ds.d.lua`, LuaDoc, examples, and runtime validation describe the same
  API.
☐ Documentation clearly distinguishes borrowed native screens, external
  overlays, and DwarfSpec-owned component screens.
☐ Documentation does not imply runner-UI implementation work.
☐ Documentation links and Mermaid diagrams, if any, render correctly.

## Phase 11: Verification and live DFHack acceptance

### Goal

Prove the feature at unit, syntax, package, and live-host layers, with cleanup
verified independently from functional assertions.

### Tasks

☐ Run focused unit tests for mount separation, adapters, native traversal,
  inspection, sources, pointer/input, render observation, and cleanup.
☐ Run the complete standalone unit suite.
☐ Run the repository Lua syntax check.
☐ Build the local rock through the supported project tool and record the
  result; do not add a static packaging-contract test.
☐ Install the locally built rock into the intended local test environment when
  needed for live validation.
☐ Run `git diff --check`.
☐ Review `git status --short` and account for every modified or added file.
☐ Create a live DwarfSpec test that captures the current native viewscreen,
  widget root, DFHack focus, screen stack, pointer/button state, and render
  dispatcher identity before attachment.
☐ In the live test, attach with `ds.mount()` and prove the root is the exact
  native `viewscreen.widgets` container.
☐ Resolve at least one named native path, one numeric native path, and one
  mixed path through `ds.get()`.
☐ If a stable live widget name containing `/` is available, prove its
  segment-array lookup; otherwise record unit evidence for that syntax and why
  the live screen lacks a stable candidate.
☐ Inspect live native bounds, direct visibility/activity, native type, and
  available text without invoking callbacks.
☐ Place the pointer on a real rendered native control before mouse input and
  prove the observed interaction through normal screen dispatch.
☐ Exercise absolute-coordinate pointer placement and restore the original
  pointer state.
☐ Register or select a test-owned overlay, resolve it with
  `ESubjectSource.OVERLAY`, and prove normal overlay input/render dispatch.
☐ Exercise top-level and subject redraw with default waiting.
☐ Exercise `{wait=false}` and prove it does not wait for a render generation.
☐ Unmount and independently verify the original viewscreen, focus, screen
  stack, pointer/button state, and render dispatcher identity.
☐ Prove no native screen or widget was dismissed and no owned-screen evidence
  was recorded.
☐ Restore test-owned overlay registration and configuration independently.
☐ Record live process state, exact commands, pass/fail output, and cleanup
  evidence; do not report a live result from unit or syntax evidence.

### Phase 11 exit gate

☐ Focused and full unit suites pass.
☐ Lua syntax, local package build, and `git diff --check` pass.
☐ Live DFHack evidence proves native lookup, inspection, input, overlay
  dispatch, redraw completion, and exact cleanup.
☐ Any environment limitation is recorded as an explicit incomplete item rather
  than being treated as passing evidence.
☐ Every earlier phase and exit gate is complete.

